>This goes back to the old discussion: Does having a game feature
>availible to gamers make the game too easy?  What if a person just
>chooses not to use it?  Does that mean it should remain for all others to
>use if they want?
>
>My take on the discussion is that certain "tricks" of the game should be
>left in.  If someone doesn't think its right, then they shouldn't use the
>trick.

I tend to agree.  Otherwise, there are many "abuses" which would need to go
away, ie (spoilers ahead):


1.  The sure way to win :-) finding a sink near an altar.  A character with
    a sufficiently high charisma (>= 15) and a bit of luck in the early going
    can, by kicking the sink, summon a nice bunch of black puddings to be
    offered up to god for glory, luck and Mjollnir, and have an unlimited
    number of educational experiences with incubi/succubi.  Level 30 here I
    come...

2.  The Guards trick.  Once you have a sufficiently strong chaotic character,
    just sit in a vault and knock off the guards.  Ultimately, you'll get that
    +7 protection ring you always wanted, as well as other cool armor and
    enough K-rations to feed the 7th Army.  This trick works best with an altar
    handy.

It could be argued that these tricks are not "abuses" in that you need a pretty
strong character to attempt them, but they have that same aspect of
"trickiness" about them in much the same way as polypiling...

